A tail call with seven parameters loses `musttail` in a release build.

The book (ch10, appendix a) promises that "a call in tail position consumes
no stack, in every engine". In a dev build every tail call below is emitted
as `musttail call tailcc`. In a `--release` build the same calls are emitted
as plain `call preserve_nonecc`, with no `musttail`, once a function has
seven parameters of which most are boxed values. Six parameters keep the
`musttail`. Whether the program then overflows depends on whether LLVM
happens to turn the recursion into a loop on its own.

This small program survives because LLVM inlines it into a loop, but the IR
shows the missing guarantee:

    kanso build main.kso --release && grep 'call.*d_tc/p[io]ng' main.ll

  should: every call to ping_7 / pong_7 is `musttail call`
  does:   `call preserve_nonecc %KValue @"d_tc/pong_7"(...)` without musttail

In the regex port the same thing happened to the Pike VM's position loop,
`scan`, which then took one stack frame per character of the subject:
`regex --steps '(a*)*b' FILE` with 40,000 a's in FILE segfaulted in the
release build (gdb: 32,452 frames of d_re/scan_7) and ran fine on the dev
build and the interpreter. The port works around it by keeping every loop
over the subject at six parameters or fewer (re/pike.kso, `search`).
